@page "/forgot-password"
@inject IApiClient Api

<PageTitle>Forgotten password - Dumb Luck Routes</PageTitle>

@*
	The way back in, for somebody who cannot sign in (§7.7). Anonymous by construction - the
	person reading this has no session, and a recovery screen behind [Authorize] would be a
	door that only opens from the inside.

	§7.8 governs what this screen is allowed to say: the answer never depends on whether the
	address belongs to an account. The server answers 202 either way - for an unknown address,
	for a rate-limited one, for a blank one - and this screen has to keep that promise, because
	a message that appeared only for real addresses would turn the form into a membership test
	for every mailbox somebody cares to type.
*@

<div class="page page-form form-page recover">
	<h1>Forgotten password</h1>

	@if (_sent)
	{
		<div class="callout">
			<strong>If that address belongs to an account, a reset link is on its way.</strong>
			It works for one hour. We cannot tell you whether it went anywhere - that would say
			who does and does not have an account here.
		</div>

		<p class="note">
			Nothing arrived? A reset link is only ever sent to an address that has been
			<em>confirmed</em>. An address that was typed and never confirmed cannot recover an
			account, and neither can an account that never had one.
		</p>

		<p><a href="/welcome">Back to sign in</a></p>
	}
	else
	{
		<p class="lead">
			Type the email address on the account. If it is there and confirmed, we will send a
			link that lets you choose a new password.
		</p>

		<form @onsubmit="SendAsync" @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
			<label>
				Email address
				<input type="email" @bind="_email" autocomplete="email" placeholder="you@example.com" required />
			</label>

			<button type="submit" class="primary" disabled="@(_busy || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_email))">
				@(_busy ? "Sending…" : "Send the link")
			</button>
		</form>

		@if (_error is not null)
		{
			<div class="error" role="alert">
				<strong>@_error.Title</strong>
				@if (_error.Messages.Count > 0)
				{
					<ul>@foreach (string message in _error.Messages) { <li>@message</li> }</ul>
				}
			</div>
		}

		<p class="note">
			An account with no email address cannot be recovered at all - that is the trade
			registration states up front (§7.2). If this is that account, there is nothing this
			screen can do.
		</p>

		<p><a href="/welcome">Back to sign in</a></p>
	}
</div>

@* AGPL §13 source offer - every page, public and authed (§14.6.2). *@
<BlazorDLR.Shared.Components.SourceOfferFooter />

@code {
	private string _email = string.Empty;
	private bool _busy;
	private bool _sent;
	private ApiError? _error;

	/// <summary>
	/// Asks for a link, and says the same thing however that went.
	/// <para>
	/// A refusal from the server is <em>not</em> surfaced as "no such address" - the endpoint
	/// does not answer that question, and the only errors it can raise are transport ones. Those
	/// are worth showing, because "your phone has no signal" is a different problem to "that
	/// address is not on an account" and the rider can act on it.
	/// </para>
	/// </summary>
	private async Task SendAsync()
	{
		if (_busy || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(_email)) return;
		_busy = true;
		_error = null;
		try
		{
			await Api.ForgotPasswordAsync(new ForgotPasswordRequest(_email.Trim()));
			_sent = true;
		}
		catch (ApiException apiException)
		{
			_error = apiException.Error;
		}
		catch (Exception exception)
		{
			_error = new ApiError(default, "Could not ask for a link.", new[] { exception.Message });
		}
		finally
		{
			_busy = false;
		}
	}
}

<style>
	/* Signed-out, like Welcome - it sits a little lower than an in-app page. */
	.recover {
		margin-top: 2rem;
	}

		.recover .error {
			color: #a91c1c;
		}

		.recover .note {
			margin-top: 1rem;
			opacity: 0.85;
		}
</style>
